  "record": {
    "read_status": "full",
    "file_problem": "none",
    "relation": "discusses",
    "tranche": "T6",
    "tranche_reason": "The paper argues that multimodal scaling is governed differently from text-only scaling, emphasizing knowledge density rather than task diversity. Its experiments compare data-supervision configurations, but do not fit or test power laws or compute-optimal allocations.",
    "gate_note": "The paper discusses scaling laws and argues for a distinct multimodal scaling principle. Its own experiments change multimodal training supervision and report benchmark scores and an estimated knowledge-density measure. These do not measure held-out cross-entropy scaling, extrapolation, compute-optimal allocations, or downstream predictability from scale or pretraining loss. The rows below record the related training and measurement results as versions [\"none\"].",
    "test_reason": "The paper discusses Kaplan/Hoffmann scaling laws and asserts that multimodal scaling is less predictable. Its own experiments compare data-supervision mixtures at a single fixed model size and token budget (about 3B params with about 100B tokens, and an unspecified model with about 50B tokens). It reports benchmark averages and an LLM-extracted knowledge-density count. There is no sweep over parameters, tokens or compute, no held-out loss, no fit or extrapolation, no allocation or shape test, and no downstream-versus-scale relation. Its claim that multimodal scaling is weaker rests on cited Qwen token counts (Table 1), not on a loss or performance-versus-scale measurement, so it is not a specific T5 result against V5. I changed the reader's T5 to T6: the paper only discusses the claim and does not test it.",
    "authors_conclusion": "Mostly correct. The authors conclude that VQA supervision adds little beyond captions: it can be removed or reconstructed from captions with negligible loss. They also conclude that raising knowledge density through paired-caption construction improves performance, and that multimodal scaling is governed more by knowledge density than by task diversity. They call knowledge density likely necessary but not sufficient for scaling. Note that 'scaling' here means capability gains from data content, not loss as a function of size or compute.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "The study changes multimodal training-data supervision and measures benchmark performance; it does not use the original scaling-law fitting or compute-optimal allocation methods.",
    "method_sensitivity": "Outcomes differed across supervision configurations and benchmark categories; the paper reports weaker results for interleaved data on most multimodal benchmarks and mixed outcomes for synthetic VQA.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "At a fixed 3B size and about 100B-token budget, removing VQA (caption-only) or reconstructing it from captions changes benchmark averages by under 0.015 across four categories.",
        "proposed_claim": "At fixed scale, VQA-format supervision adds little beyond captions for MLLM benchmark performance.",
        "row_ids": [
          "r1",
          "r2",
          "r3",
          "r4",
          "r5",
          "r6",
          "r7",
          "r8"
        ]
      },
      {
        "finding": "Replacing captions with semantically paired captions while keeping VQA (Pair-Caption-v2) raised averages on all four categories, e.g. business multimodal 0.397 to 0.440. Interleaved data lowered both multimodal averages.",
        "proposed_claim": "At fixed scale, data construction that adds semantic content can improve MLLM benchmark scores more than changing the task format does.",
        "row_ids": [
          "r9",
          "r10",
          "r11",
          "r12",
